To stand out in a senior or mid-level Spring Boot interview, you need to transition from "quick definitions" to answers that show you understand internal mechanics, real-world trade-offs, and production gotchas.
Here is the comprehensive playbook on how to structure and deliver detailed, high-impact answers for each of your interview topics.

Part 1: Core Framework Mechanics (Questions 1–4)


1. How does auto-configuration work?

• The Framework Concept: Spring Boot auto-configuration is an opinionated mechanism that automatically configures your Spring application based on the dependencies present on your classpath.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• During startup, the framework scans META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports found within your starter JARs.
	• It loads a list of fully qualified candidate configuration classes.
	• Each candidate class evaluates a series of conditional annotations (e.g., @ConditionalOnClass, @ConditionalOnMissingBean, @ConditionalOnProperty).
• Production Context / Trade-off: Explicit user-defined beans always take priority. If you declare your own DataSource bean, @ConditionalOnMissingBean ensures the auto-configured one backs off. You can debug this lifecycle using --debug or the Actuator /conditions endpoint.

2. Difference between @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller?

• The Framework Concept: All four are stereotype annotations meta-annotated with @Component, meaning they make the class eligible for automatic classpath scanning and bean creation. Their core difference is semantic intent and specialized post-processing.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• @Component: The generic stereotype for any Spring-managed utility or component.
	• @Service: Represents business logic. It holds no extra technical behavior over @Component but defines architectural intent.
	• @Repository: Encapsulates storage, retrieval, and search behavior. It triggers Spring’s PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor, automatically translating low-level JDBC/JPA/Hibernate SQLExceptions into Spring’s portable DataAccessException hierarchy.
	• @Controller: Marks a class as a web handler. The DispatcherServlet scans these to detect request mappings (@RequestMapping).

3. @Controller vs @RestController?

• The Framework Concept: @RestController is a convenience stereotype that simplifies building RESTful web services.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• @RestController is meta-annotated with both @Controller and @ResponseBody.
	• In a classic @Controller, handler methods return a String representing a view name (like a Thymeleaf template) unless you manually add @ResponseBody to the method.
	• In a @RestController, @ResponseBody is implicitly applied to every method. Spring passes the return object directly to an HttpMessageConverter (like Jackson for JSON) to write directly into the HTTP response body.

4. @Bean vs @Component?

• The Framework Concept: Both register beans in the ApplicationContext, but they target different use cases based on source code ownership.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• @Component is a class-level annotation used for automatic discovery via component scanning. You use this on source code that you own and can modify.
	• @Bean is a method-level annotation used inside a @Configuration class. It tells Spring that the object returned by this method should be registered as a bean. You use this when you need to instantiate and configure third-party libraries (e.g., configuring an ObjectMapper or a RedisTemplate), or when bean creation requires complex, explicit initialization logic.

Part 2: Configuration & Startup (Questions 5–7 & 10)


5. How do you change the embedded server?

• The Framework Concept: Spring Boot uses spring-boot-starter-web which includes Tomcat by default. Changing it requires mutating the dependency tree.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• In your build file (pom.xml or build.gradle), you explicitly exclude spring-boot-starter-tomcat from your web starter dependency.
	• You then explicitly include an alternative starter, such as spring-boot-starter-jetty or spring-boot-starter-undertow.
• Production Context / Trade-off: Undertow is frequently preferred in high-concurrency microservices due to its extremely low memory footprint and non-blocking architecture options compared to Tomcat.

6. How do you change the default port?

• The Framework Concept: The default port is 8080, which can be overridden via property resolution order.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• Property files: Set server.port=9090 in application.properties or application.yml.
	• Command line: Pass it as an argument during execution: --server.port=9090.
	• Environment variable: Set SERVER_PORT=9090 (ideal for Docker containers).
	• Dynamic allocation: Setting server.port=0 instructs Spring Boot to look for a random, unallocated ephemeral port. This is heavily used in local integration testing or microservice clusters where ports shouldn't conflict.

7. How to run code at startup?

• The Framework Concept: Spring provides multiple lifecycle hooks that execute logic immediately after the application context initializes.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• @PostConstruct: A standard Jakarta annotation. Executes right after the specific bean's dependency injection is complete, but before the application fully starts accepting traffic.
	• CommandLineRunner / ApplicationRunner: Spring interfaces. They run code after the entire ApplicationContext is fully refreshed. CommandLineRunner gives you raw string arguments, while ApplicationRunner wraps them in an ApplicationArguments object.
	• ApplicationReadyEvent: A decoupled event approach. You create an EventListener for this event, which fires only when the application is fully ready to service HTTP requests.

10. How do you manage environment-specific config?

• The Framework Concept: I utilize Spring Profiles combined with the 12-Factor App methodology of externalizing configurations.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• Define base values in application.yml, and override them using profile-specific files like application-dev.yml or application-prod.yml.
	• Activate them at runtime via -Dspring.profiles.active=prod.
• Production Context / Trade-off: In enterprise Kubernetes environments, I do not hardcode secrets in profile files. Instead, I use environment variables placeholder configurations (database.password: ${DB_PASSWORD}) injected into the container via Kubernetes Secrets or centralized configuration managers like HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager.

