11. Difference between @RequestParam and @PathVariable?

• The Framework Concept: Both extract values from an incoming HTTP request, but they map to different parts of the URL structure.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• @PathVariable extracts values directly embedded into the URI path template (e.g., /users/{id}). It is typically used to identify a specific resource in RESTful designs.
	• @RequestParam extracts query parameters appended after the ? character (e.g., /users?role=admin) or reads form-encoded POST data. It is ideally used for filtering, sorting, or paginating resource collections.

12. How does @Transactional work internally?

• The Framework Concept: Spring handles transactions using Spring AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) proxies.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• When a bean marked with @Transactional is injected, Spring does not inject the raw bean; it injects a dynamically generated proxy object.
	• When a method is called, the proxy intercepts the invocation and uses a PlatformTransactionManager (e.g., JpaTransactionManager) to open a database connection and start a transaction.
	• If the target method completes successfully, the proxy commits the transaction.
	• If the method throws a RuntimeException or Error, the proxy triggers a rollback.
• Gotcha: Self-invocation (calling a @Transactional method from another method inside the same class) bypasses the proxy entirely, causing the transaction configuration to be ignored.

13. Why constructor injection?

• The Framework Concept: Constructor injection is the industry-standard best practice, vastly superior to field injection (@Autowired on variables).
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• Immutability: It allows dependencies to be declared as final, guaranteeing they cannot be modified post-initialization.
	• Fail-Fast Initialization: It prevents NullPointerExceptions because the application will refuse to start if a mandatory dependency is missing.
	• Testing: It decouples code from the Spring container; you can instantiate the class in a unit test using plain Java new MyService(mockRepo).
	• Circular Dependency Detection: It surfaces cyclic dependencies immediately at application startup rather than hidden until runtime.

16. Singleton bean thread-safety?

• The Framework Concept: By default, Spring beans are singletons—meaning exactly one instance exists per ApplicationContext. This instance is shared across all concurrent worker threads handling incoming requests.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• Singleton does not mean thread-safe. If your bean contains mutable instance variables (state), it is highly vulnerable to race conditions and data corruption.
• Production Context / Trade-off: The standard practice is to write stateless beans. Beans should only contain read-only configuration attributes or references to other stateless beans. Any operational data should be passed via local method parameters, which are allocated to the thread's own stack and are inherently thread-safe.

17. What happens if two beans match a type?

• The Framework Concept: If you attempt to inject a dependency by type and Spring finds multiple implementing beans, the container throws a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException at startup.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics: To resolve this ambiguity, you must guide the framework:
	• @Primary: Designates a default bean that takes precedence when multiple options match.
	• @Qualifier("beanName"): Explicitly names the exact bean you want to wire at the injection point.
	• Variable Names: Spring also uses the variable name as a fallback qualifier matching strategy.

18. How do you implement pagination?

• The Framework Concept: In Spring Data, pagination is achieved out-of-the-box by passing a Pageable object into your repository layer.
• Deep-Dive Mechanics:
	• Your controller accepts a Pageable parameter (which automatically binds query parameters like ?page=0&size=20&sort=name,desc).
	• Your repository method signature accepts this Pageable object and returns a Page<T> or Slice<T>.
• Production Context / Trade-off: Returning a Page<T> executes an implicit, expensive count query alongside the data query to calculate total elements. For massive datasets where total count isn't strictly necessary for the UI (like infinite scroll), I use Slice<T> instead, which only fetches size + 1 records to check if a next page exists, saving significant database performance overhead.

